  "answers": {
    "netmedex_hybrid_rag": "**Direct evidence for the first hop (chronic hepatitis B → liver cirrhosis)**  \nA large multicenter analysis of 6 682 patients with metabolic‑associated steatotic liver disease (MASLD) showed that concurrent chronic hepatitis B (CHB) independently raised the odds of significant fibrosis, advanced fibrosis and cirrhosis (adjusted odds ratios 1.98‑3.73; p < 0.001)【PMID: 41997336】. This human cohort provides mechanistic support that CHB accelerates fibrotic progression to cirrhosis.\n\n**Direct evidence for the second hop (liver cirrhosis → hepatocellular carcinoma)**  \nIn a multicenter French cohort of 354 MASLD‑related HCC cases, cirrhosis was present in 65 % of patients and was strongly linked to HCC development; cirrhotic patients were more often diagnosed through screening and exhibited poorer survival compared with non‑cirrhotic cases, confirming cirrhosis as a key risk factor for HCC【PMID: 39411648】.\n\n**Overall mechanistic inference (CHB → cirrhosis → HCC)**  \nIntegrating the two hops yields a coherent pathway: chronic hepatitis B infection increases liver cirrhosis, and cirrhosis in turn is associated with hepatocellular carcinoma. The graph‑derived claim formalizes this cascade (see below).\n\n**Graph‑derived candidate insights**  \n\n- **GRAPH‑CONFIRMED** [PATH 7e9fef4360d43ca1] hepatitis b chronic, increases, liver cirrhosis, associated_with, carcinoma hepatocellular [PMID: 41997336, 39411648].\n\n**Conclusion**  \nHuman cohort data demonstrate that chronic hepatitis B promotes cirrhosis, and cirrhosis markedly raises HCC risk, supporting a mechanistic CHB → cirrhosis → HCC pathway."
  },
